VI(1)                      UNIX Programmer's Manual                      VI(1)

NNAAMMEE
       vi - visual text editor

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       vvii [--eeiiRRvv] [++_c_m_d] [_f_i_l_e ...]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       _v_i is the visual screen editor.  On FGX it is provided by _e_l_v_i_s, a
       compact clone of the Berkeley _v_i/_e_x editor by Steve Kirkendall.  It
       opens each _f_i_l_e in turn for interactive editing and, on write, replaces
       the file with the edited text.

       _v_i has two principal modes.  In ccoommmmaanndd mode, keystrokes are editing
       commands (move the cursor, delete, change, yank, put).  In iinnppuutt mode
       -- entered with ii, aa, oo, and friends -- keystrokes are inserted into
       the text; press EESSCC to return to command mode.  A line beginning with ::
       introduces an _e_x command (::ww, ::qq, ::wwqq, ::ss, ::rr, ::!!, and so on); the
       command line is read at the bottom of the screen and executed when
       RREETTUURRNN is pressed.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --RR     Set the rreeaaddoonnllyy option so a file is not accidentally
              overwritten.

       --ee     Start in _e_x (colon command) mode rather than visual mode.

       --vv     Start in visual command mode (the default).

       --ii     Start in input mode.

       ++_c_m_d   After the first file is loaded, execute _c_m_d as an _e_x command.
              vvii ++223377 ffoooo edits _f_o_o and jumps to line 237.

FFGGXX NNOOTTEESS
       The FGX console is a vvtt110000 terminal (TTEERRMM=vvtt110000); the kernel emits
       vt100 escape sequences for the arrow keys system-wide.  In _v_i the arrow
       keys move the cursor as hh, jj, kk, and ll in command and input modes.  On
       the :: command line -- which is append/erase only and has no cursor
       motion -- the arrow keys are inert rather than corrupting the line.

       The DDeelleettee key is a destructive backspace: it erases the character it
       removes, both while typing text and on the :: command line.  CCoonnttrrooll--HH,
       CCoonnttrrooll--UU, and the erase/kill characters set by _s_t_t_y(1) behave as
       usual.

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
       TTEERRMM   The terminal type; normally vvtt110000 on FGX.

       SSHHEELLLL  The shell used for ::!! filters and wildcard expansion.  The
              default is //bbiinn//sshh.

       HHOOMMEE   _v_i reads its initialization file (..eexxrrcc) from this directory, if
              set.

       EEXXIINNIITT _e_x commands executed before any ..eexxrrcc file.

FFIILLEESS
       //ttmmpp//eellvv**
              Temporary edit buffer, one per edited file.

       ..eexxrrcc  Per-user (home and current directory) startup _e_x commands.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
       _e_x(1), _e_d(1), _s_e_d(1), _s_t_t_y(1)

BBUUGGSS
       Long lines are scrolled sideways rather than wrapped onto multiple
       screen rows.  There is no LISP support, and auto-indent is not quite
       _v_i-compatible.

AAUUTTHHOORR
       Steve Kirkendall (elvis).  Ported to FGX as a multi-bank userland
       editor.

